PN={
'luna-park':'The 1930 USGS map shows a trolley spur leaving the main line north of the bridge and ending at this spot, right on the Schenectady–Saratoga county line. LiDAR shows rectangular platforms on the terrace about 100 m north (42.8543, -73.8945). Turn on the old-map overlay and the LiDAR layer to walk it before you go.',
'trolley-bridge-piers':'Piers show on the leaf-off aerial in a line at about -73.8994, roughly half a mile upstream of the Route 146 bridge. The 1930 map shows the trolley crossing here.',
'overlook-mountain-house':'The roofless concrete shell is plainly visible on the leaf-off aerial at this spot, beside the carriage road.',
'hotel-kaaterskill':'The 1903 map draws the hotel here. Today it is a clearing with a small pond; LiDAR shows low platforms and a long wall line just northwest.',
'scott-ice-house':'The ice house\'s big rectangular platform shows on LiDAR at the river\'s edge, matching the marker location.',
'lock-23':'The lock chamber shows on LiDAR just south of where the trail crosses.',
'waterford-side-cut':'Three old lock chambers in a row are visible on the aerial, immediately south of Barge Canal Lock 2.',
'schoharie-crossing':'The surviving arches are visible on the aerial crossing Schoharie Creek at this point.',
'empire-brickyard':'The 1929 USGS map draws a long kiln shed on the east shore here, between Little Nutten Hook and Gays Point. Not yet matched to a field photo.',
'gilboa':'The 1901 USGS map shows the village centered here, just upstream of today\'s dam.',
'forest-park':'The 1930 map shows the trolley along the west shore with buildings between the track and the lake at the south end. The park covered 144 acres, so treat the pin as the middle of it.',
'electric-park':'LiDAR shows the straight trolley embankment just east of the shore; Electric Park Road runs to the lobed peninsulas where the park stood.',
'kaydeross-park':'The mid-century USGS map labels "Kaydeross Park" at the lakeshore here; the 1934 map shows buildings at the same spot.',
'rensselaer-park':'The 1929 map shows open ground here at the east edge of the street grid, between 108th and 110th streets. Today it is the school campus and playing fields.',
'brandywine-park':'The 1930 map shows an open block here, southwest of Albany Street opposite Elm Street, which matches the Historical Society\'s description of the gate.',
'prospect-park-pool':'The oval above-ground pool is visible on the aerial at the south end of the park.',
'catskill-mountain-house':'The 1903 map\'s building symbol matches the cliff-edge clearing visible on the aerial.',
'kenwood':'The cleared building site is visible on the aerial uphill from South Pearl Street, where the USGS map prints "Sacred Heart."',
'otis-incline':'The 1903 map draws the incline as a straight line from Otis Junction to the Mountain House; the pin sits on the lower grade.',
'matton-shipyard':'Shipyard buildings and slips are visible on the aerial between Delaware Avenue and the river.',
'poestenkill-gorge':'Pin is at the falls and lower gorge, where the mill ruins are. The gorge and old raceway benches show clearly on LiDAR.',
'burr-bridge':'Pin is where Washington Avenue meets the river bank. Not confirmed stone by stone on imagery.',
'rexford-aqueduct':'Pin is on the south bank just east of the Route 146 bridge, where the two surviving arches stand; the county marker is 170 m south on the bike trail.',
'mid-city-park':'The 1928 map shows open ground here between Broadway and a pond, in the year Hawkins Stadium opened.',
'al-tro-park':'The 1928 map still shows the island here, cut off by the Erie Canal on the west. The pin is the trail marker.',
'knox-cave':'Pin is the roadside marker, not the cave entrance.',
'hammondville':'The 1897 map shows buildings and the end of the Crown Point Iron Company railroad in this hollow. Spot not confirmed on the ground.',
'graphite':'The 1897 map prints "Graphite" at the road junction just northwest of the pin.',
'griffin':'The 1910 map shows the cluster of buildings and the name here, by the bridge over the East Branch.',
'sacandaga-park':'The 1899 map shows the park\'s street grid at the station and the river flats to the east that the reservoir later covered.',
'browns-station':'Approximate. Use the 1901 map overlay to find the Ulster & Delaware station before trusting this pin.',
'laurel-house':'Pin is at the top of the falls. The 1903 map marks the Laurel House station on the railroad about 200 m northwest.',
'grand-hotel':'The 1903 map draws the long hotel building across this spot.',
'tremper-house':'Approximate: pinned near the Phoenicia railroad station. The 1903 map does not label the hotel.',
'dibbles-quarry':'Approximate. Not marked on old maps; follow the trail description.',
'stuyvesant-light':'Approximate; not confirmed on a chart.',
'cohoes-locks':'Pin is the canal map marker, not a lock. Lock 16 is behind Harmony Mill No. 1 near Vliet and Harmony streets (42.7792, -73.7053).',
'crescent-aqueduct':'Approximate: pinned at Crescent Park on the north bank by the Route 9 bridge. Abutment stones not located on imagery.',
'burden-wheel':'Approximate: pinned in the Wynants Kill gorge between Burden Pond and Mill Street. The 1928 map shows works buildings just west.',
'the-hill':'Approximate: pinned between the county hockey rink and the jail. No map of the burials is known.',
'coxsackie-light':'Pinned at the Coast Guard\'s rounded position (42°22.5′ N, 73°47.4′ W), which is only good to a few hundred metres. The station stood at the north tip of Rattlesnake Island.',
'fort-orange':'Pinned at the documented site under the highway ramps, not at the marker.',
}
EXTRA_LOOK={
'al-tro-park':' Dates conflict between sources: the trail marker gives Al-Tro as 1906–1909, followed on the same site by Maple Beach Park (1910–1912) and Midway Beach Park (1915–1921); another source has it opening as Al-Tro Island Park on Memorial Day 1907. Check which park the 1913 fire belongs to before you say it on camera.',
'luna-park':' The park went by Luna Park (1906), Dolle\'s Park (1911), Palisades Park (1916) and Rexford Park. George Farrall\'s booklet "The Amusement Park at Rexford" (2003) and the Clifton Park library\'s Farrall Collection have the photographs.',
'forest-park':' The inn and the dining hall survive as private homes, and one original outhouse still stands. A wooden causeway once crossed the swamp at the lake\'s south end, and you can still see its line. The county historians have run walking tours of the grounds.',
}
